<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Channel Islands</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Channel Islands are an archipelago off the coast of Normandy, comprising the Bailiwicks of Jersey and Guernsey, the latter including Alderney and Sark. Once part of the Duchy of Normandy, they stayed with the English crown after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> lost mainland Normandy in 1204. They are British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-dependencies">Crown Dependencies</a>, not part of the United Kingdom, and were the only British territory occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/channel-islands.jpg" alt="Channel Islands" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Copernicus Sentinel-2, ESA · CC BY-SA 3.0 igo · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Channel_Islands_by_Sentinel-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Channel Islands are an archipelago in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a>, to the west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotentin-peninsula">Cotentin Peninsula</a> and off the Normandy coast. Channel Islands is a geographical term, not a political unit, first used around 1830, possibly by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>. The two major islands, Jersey and Guernsey, make up 99 per cent of the population and 92 per cent of the area. The highest point is Les Platons in Jersey at 143 metres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Humans lived on what is now Jersey at least 250,000 to 200,000 years ago, as La Cotte de St Brelade shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neanderthal">Neanderthal</a> occupation when the island was connected to the mainland. Rising sea levels detached the islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> period. Dolmens, the burial mound of La Hougue Bie in Jersey and the statue menhirs of Guernsey show a population large and organised enough for constructions of considerable size. In the sixth century Christian missionaries visited, and saints such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samson">Samson</a> of Dol and Helier are associated with the islands.</p><p>In 933 Raoul, King of Western Francia, granted the islands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-longsword">William Longsword</a>, and they were annexed to the Duchy of Normandy. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> lost mainland Normandy to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a>, he kept control of the islands, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1259">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1259 Henry III surrendered his claim to the duchy while retaining them. Since around 1290 they have been split into two bailiwicks. In the Wars of the Three Kingdoms Jersey held out for the Royalist cause and gave refuge to Charles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>, while Presbyterian Guernsey favoured Parliament. Charles II rewarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-carteret">George Carteret</a>, bailiff and governor of Jersey, with a grant of land in America, which he named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The islands form two self-governing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-dependencies">Crown Dependencies</a>, the Bailiwick of Jersey and the Bailiwick of Guernsey, which includes Alderney, Sark and Herm. Neither is part of the United Kingdom, which is responsible for their defence and international relations. Each has its own laws, elections and representative bodies. The capitals are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-helier">Saint Helier</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-peter-port">Saint Peter Port</a>. German forces occupied the islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>: the British government demilitarised them in June 1940, and 6,600 of 50,000 people left Jersey and 17,000 of 42,000 left Guernsey, among them thousands of children evacuated with their schools.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Channel Islands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jersey, the largest of the <b>Channel Islands</b> at about 118 square kilometres, lies some 22 kilometres off the Cotentin peninsula of Normandy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bailiwick-of-jersey">Bailiwick of Jersey · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Channel Islands</b> are the last part of the old Duchy of Normandy still held by the British crown, and their law still rests partly on Norman custom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Sark in the <b>Channel Islands</b>, settled in 1565 by Helier de Carteret under a charter from Elizabeth I and divided into forty tenements whose holders sat in its parliament, kept what was called Europe’s last feudal system until 2008.</blockquote><a href="/government/feudalism">Feudalism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Like the other <b>Channel Islands</b> it remained a possession of the crown in its role as Duke of Normandy, never part of the Kingdom of England.</blockquote><a href="/state/alderney">Alderney</a></li></ul></section>
